ral order, in which longer is better: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"MaxPlusSemiring(9) < MaxPlusSemiring(2)"},{"text":". The order","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"is the reverse of the values’, so ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"[a, b].max()"},{"text":" is the shorter and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".min()"},{"text":" the longer.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"⊗ is a closed sum. −∞ (the zero) absorbs every value, and any other sum traps on integer","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"overflow. A finite integer sum that lands exactly on ","type":"text"},{"code":".min","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the zero, so an edge of that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"weight carries no path. The carrier is ℝ ∪ {−∞}, without +∞. No operation checks for NaN. The","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"ShortestPaths entry points trap on a NaN weight.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Longest paths exist where no cycle has a positive length: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"dagShortestPaths"},{"type":"text","text":" gives them in an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"acyclic graph, and Bellman–Ford returns "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" when a positive cycle is reachable. Dijkstra’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"algorithm needs every examined weight to be at most 0."}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["dag.dagShortestPaths(from: s) { MaxPlusSemiring(duration[$0]) }"],"syntax":null}]}],"relationshipsSections":[{"type":"conformsTo","title":"Conforms To","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Semirings\/documentation\/Semirings\/SelectiveSemiring","doc:\/\/Semirings\/documentation\/Semirings\/Semiring","doc:\/\/Semirings\/SL","doc:\/\/Semirings\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/Semirings\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/Semirings\/SQ","doc:\/\/Semiring